# Requests development caller

This added development study uses the two natural public Requests objectives
5414 then6028. It does not replace the original family, horizon, warm/cold,
mechanism or generalization requirements. Neither Requests nor these qualification
controls constitute a new held-out sample.

Every arm starts from its own source/event namespace and fresh local stores.
The frozen sequential order is the SHA256 ordering of D,C,A,B under
`ns-requests-cold-development-20260914-v1`. The shared journal holds effect and cost
records; accepted roots are keyed by trajectory, native world stores by
trajectory, native seal repositories by trajectory, and source/context/candidate
artifacts by event. A model receives only that arm's prior public outcomes.
Provider-side prompt caching is uncontrolled and must be reported from usage.

A uses ordinary lexical whole-module retrieval and an ordinary current check.
B additionally prepares and verifies a new full public native context, then uses
an exact raw fallback: this repository profile qualifies zero source omissions.
C additionally invokes actual IPA, bounded native CEGIS and native routing; every
candidate requires the same repository grader. In controller v2 native dispatch
and publication halt decisions constrain the caller. All eligible model tiers
map to the same qualified Grok4.6 profile, so model-tier selection savings are not
identified. D additionally calls the qualified native memory and repository
publication services; it is partial D because lifecycle reuse, certified
procedures and learned world utility are absent.

Each trajectory has at most four provider requests, with at most two on its first
node and unused capacity available on the second. Proposal, source expansion,
retry and merge resolution all consume that same allowance. The finite catalog
contains96 conditional IDs; the controller's trajectory budget allows at most16
actual posts over the four trajectories. No model is forced to run when current
or native checks close a task. All arms use Grok4.6 low,2048 output tokens,
240-second transport bounds and65536-byte requests. Initial retrieval selects at
most3 whole modules totaling48000 raw bytes. Retrieval scores issue text before
the conventional system-information footer, while prompts retain the entire
original issue byte sequence, including CRLF. Expansion replaces the source
selection with requested existing production modules. It never reveals hidden
checks or evaluation logs. Exact replacement edits use path/old/new and require a
unique old string. Only existing public production modules are in edit scope; model outputs contain
bounded exact replacements.

Both public exports reproduce their actual upstream Git tree identities. If5414
is accepted, the caller creates a real accepted-source commit parented by that
public base and performs Git's three-way merge with the public6028 base.
Conflicts remain visible and any neural resolution consumes the same allowance.
The submitted6028 patch is the entire resulting production diff against6028's
pristine base. Source modes are normalized to the scorer's observed git-apply
umask: untouched files preserve pristine modes and changed regular files use
0644 or0755 according to Git executable status. If5414 fails, the upstream update
can advance the public input, but its earlier failure and costs remain in the
trajectory. Event predecessor and accepted-parent identities are separate.

An incomplete/error native grade ends an event as `infrastructure_unresolved`
before a repair inference or retry. Complete failed grades allow bounded ordinary
retry. Candidate grading success and acceptance/publication success are distinct
fields; end-to-end accepted state is the primary endpoint. Unknown provider
requests occupy a conservative budget slot and retain null calls/tokens where
remote work cannot be determined. Existing terminal requests and unknown effects
are never silently repeated.

Native repository sealing v1 stores the exact full source, the source-bound
native context, the complete observed **non-sealing** controller effect inventory
and the native behavior receipt. Its effect leaf explicitly excludes all
`seal_*` effects, including prior native reads. Parent/source bindings are
independently verified through the native chain and full source checks. The outer
workflow ledger must account for every native read, publication and recovery;
exclusion from a proof leaf does not remove its cost. Admission invokes an actual
fresh ordinary native grader replay. These are unsigned local integrity receipts,
not theorem proofs or adversarial oracle-integrity guarantees. Every required leaf
is rebuilt/replaced; no avoided proof-leaf or test-body work is claimed.

Replay appearances in responses are not dispatch counts: the service can return a
saved replay completion for the same operation ID. Count actual dispatch intents
and distinct evaluation IDs, retain unknown attempts, and include fresh recover
and read IDs. Controller wall time includes its services; service and replay
wall times are nested components and must not be added again. Priming and all
context preparation are charged explicitly to the relevant runtime or separate
qualification ledger. CPU/container costs not observed remain unavailable.

Provider-enabled controller v2 requires a passed, hash-bound curator publication
qualification and exactly matching service/helper and actual native source bytes
before any provider dispatch or native seal operation. Curator positive controls
use separate candidate roots, context/native stores and evidence custody. No
reference source, patch, context or history becomes a live proposer input.

The first provider-disabled attempts remain preserved. Offline v2's concurrent
launcher exposed a shared journal sequence race and two omitted optional native
world imports; the live protocol is sequential and later enrollments include those
imports. A/B completed and produced byte-identical first prompts after actual B
native context work. Offline v3 completed D's real native candidate attempt, then
stopped on a changed caller source pin when resume/infrastructure fixes were
introduced. Offline v4 retains the corrected sequential caller results. These
attempts are development engineering costs, not extra repair-quality samples.

`repository-runtime-controls-v2` contains126 passing public-only controls,
including actual Git carry/conflicts and exact source/mode roundtrips, retained
candidate reconstruction, hidden-field projection and terminal/unknown effects.
`repository-contract-controls-v1` contains three declared-response branch
controls; its transport boundary cannot access the network. They confirm no
proposal follows an incomplete grade, wrong-tree passed receipts are rejected,
and unknown provider effects stay null without repeat. These stubbed controls do
not claim native task success. `repository-route-controls-v2` covers eight closed
routing admission cases. Actual publication controls are curator-owned and must
be linked by the final frozen qualification artifact.

The completed `requests-cold-v1` cohort remains immutable. All arms accepted5414;
none accepted6028. A/C/D carried their actual first repair into a Git conflict,
then stopped before dispatch because optional lexical modules plus the appended
complete conflict module made a91,202-byte wire request. B's different accepted
first repair merged without conflict, and all three subsequent proposals received
complete failing native grades. The cohort used7provider requests/108,981tokens
and16distinct native grader invocations. B5414 finished after the original driver
was interrupted; its exact controller timer is known, but outer invocation wall,
waited CPU and exit code remain unavailable. No event or provider effect was
restarted during reconciliation. These heterogeneous failures and uncontrolled
remote caching prevent a general or causal token-savings interpretation.

Prospective controller v3 uses `repository_retrieval_v1.py`: complete mandatory
conflict modules are selected first, then optional whole modules in the original
lexical order only while all three initial bounds hold:3files,48,000raw UTF8 bytes,
and65,536bytes for the actual serialized provider request. Mandatory-only overflow
remains `source_context_unresolved`; required modules are never truncated or
dropped. Exact expansion retains the existing maximum8requested paths and wire
bound, replaces optional selection, and preserves complete unresolved conflict
modules. Expansion and retry checks include actual feedback in wire size. Source
paths, symlink ancestors and exact bytes/hashes are checked before optional
admission. Every arm gets the same policy. No prior model output or accepted state
is imported into the fresh live cohort.

The successor has99public source/wire boundary controls,7actual caller/carry
controls with providers disabled and grader invocation forbidden, and3declared
infrastructure/unknown-response controls. These are admission evidence rather
than new repair outcomes. A fresh profile records that the retriever was adapted
after the first cohort, so its outcomes cannot be treated as an independent
confirmatory replication. The new finite driver is detached from the interactive
tool and records its invocation start and worker PID before waiting; all missing
future measurements must still remain unknown.
